<article class="post-body">

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── §01 -->
<section id="s-quadratic" data-code="quadratic">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Quadratic Wall</div>
<h2>Why ViT Attention Breaks at High Resolution</h2>

<p>
  Vision Transformers divide an image into a grid of N non-overlapping patches.
  Standard self-attention lets <strong>every patch attend to every other patch</strong>:
  N patches × N patches = N² pairwise interactions. For a 256-pixel image with 16-pixel patches,
  N = 256 and N² = 65,536 — manageable. But for 512 pixels, N = 1,024 and N² = 1,048,576.
  At 1024 pixels N = 4,096 and N² = 16,777,216. Every doubling of resolution multiplies FLOPs by four.
</p>

<div class="compare-grid">
  <div class="compare-panel">
    <div class="compare-label red">Standard ViT (All-to-All)</div>
    <div class="compare-stat" style="color:#ef4444">N²</div>
    <div class="compare-sub">interactions per attention layer</div>
  </div>
  <div class="compare-panel">
    <div class="compare-label green">VECA (Core-Periphery)</div>
    <div class="compare-stat" style="color:var(--green)">2NC + C²</div>
    <div class="compare-sub">interactions per attention layer</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  At 512px resolution with C = 64 cores, VECA needs 2 × 1024 × 64 + 64² = 135,168 interactions —
  <strong>87.1% fewer</strong> than the 1,048,576 of standard self-attention.
  The key claim: this linear-time approximation loses almost nothing in representation quality.
</p>

<!-- Interactive: FLOPs vs resolution comparison canvas -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">attention FLOPs vs resolution — all-to-all vs core-periphery</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag slider</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="flops-canvas" height="220"></canvas>
    <div class="vp-slider">
      <span>Patch size P =</span>
      <input type="range" id="patch-slider" min="8" max="32" step="8" value="16">
      <span id="patch-label">16 px</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-cap" id="flops-cap">At 512px with P=16: standard = 1,048,576 ops  |  VECA (C=64) = 135,168 ops  (87.1% fewer)</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Prior efficient-attention methods (sparse windows, low-rank approximations, linear attention)
  try to <em>approximate</em> the quadratic matrix while preserving its structure.
  VECA takes a different approach: replace it entirely with a structurally different
  <strong>core-periphery</strong> attention graph — patches only talk to each other indirectly,
  mediated by the cores.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── §02 -->
<section id="s-core" data-code="core">
<div class="section-no">02 — Core-Periphery Attention</div>
<h2>Patches Never Talk Directly</h2>

<p>
  VECA maintains two types of tokens in every layer: <strong>N patch tokens</strong>
  (the standard image patches) and <strong>C core tokens</strong> (learned from scratch,
  resolution-invariant). The attention matrix is block-sparse:
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Core → Full</strong>: each core token attends to all N patches <em>and</em> all other cores. Cores aggregate global information.</li>
  <li><strong>Patch → Cores only</strong>: each patch token attends only to the C cores. Patches receive global context through the core interface, never directly from other patches.</li>
  <li><strong>Patch → Patch: disabled</strong>. The N × N block of the attention matrix is zeroed out entirely.</li>
</ul>

<!-- Architecture SVG: the block-sparse attention matrix -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">VECA attention block — core-periphery structure</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover cells to see what attends to what</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <svg id="attn-svg" viewBox="0 0 540 300" style="width:100%;max-width:540px;display:block;margin:0 auto" class="attn-matrix-svg">
      <!-- Background -->
