  <span class="math-var">VECA:</span>          O(2NC + C²)    <span class="math-comment">— e.g. N=1024, C=64 → 135,168 pairs</span>
  <span class="math-var">saving:</span>        87.1%          <span class="math-comment">at 512px resolution</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── §03 -->
<section id="s-elastic" data-code="elastic">
<div class="section-no">03 — Elastic Inference</div>
<h2>Dial the Compute at Test Time</h2>

<p>
  The VECA model maintains a <strong>bank of M = 64 core tokens</strong>, ordered by importance.
  During training, a budget C is sampled from a distribution and only the first C cores
  (the "active prefix") are used. This is nested dropout applied to the core axis:
  core r₁ is active for <em>every</em> training budget, so it learns to encode the most essential
  visual information. Core r₂ is active whenever C ≥ 2. Cores at the tail specialize in fine-grained details.
</p>

<p>
  The result: at <strong>inference time</strong>, you can vary C from 8 to 64 without retraining.
  C = 8 gives faster, cheaper inference for global tasks.
  C = 64 gives maximum fidelity for dense prediction at high resolution.
</p>

<div class="callout">
  <b>Key insight:</b> classification accuracy is nearly flat across budgets — C = 8 already hits 79.99%
  ImageNet-1K (C = 64 gives 81.93%). But dense prediction keeps improving with more cores:
  C = 8 gives 53.30 mIoU on PASCAL Context vs 57.46 at C = 64. Global recognition
  needs only a few good cores; dense spatial understanding needs more.
</div>

