<p>
  The nested training also produces a useful side effect: the active cores exhibit increasingly
  coarse-to-fine spatial behavior as C grows. With C = 8 each core covers a large, coarse region.
  With C = 64 the coverage becomes fine-grained and semantically specific — visible in the
  core-to-patch attention maps.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── §04 -->
<section id="s-emergent" data-code="emergent">
<div class="section-no">04 — Emergent Semantics</div>
<h2>Core Tokens Organize Themselves Without Supervision</h2>

<p>
  The core tokens are not explicitly supervised — they receive no segmentation masks,
  no object labels, no spatial layout signal beyond RoPE positional encoding.
  Yet the paper reports a striking emergent behavior across layers:
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Layer 3 (early):</strong> core token attention maps are <em>isotropic</em> — diffuse spherical blobs with no clear semantic meaning. Each core attends to a roughly uniform region of the image.</li>
  <li><strong>Layer 12 (deep):</strong> core tokens have clustered into <em>semantically coherent groups</em>. Different cores specialize to distinct objects: sky, foreground objects, background textures, edges. They look like learned object-centric slots — without any recurrence (unlike Slot Attention).</li>
</ul>

<p>
  The paper describes this as "isotropic-to-semantic clustering" and shows it with UMAP visualizations
  of core token representations. As depth increases, the UMAP cloud transitions from a diffuse sphere
  to tight semantic clusters — one cluster per semantic category attended to.
</p>

