<p>
  Additionally, VECA paper shows that cores exhibit <strong>complementary visual roles</strong>:
  different cores attend to distinct non-overlapping spatial regions of the image (eggs vs. bowl vs. background).
  This complementarity arises from the competition induced by patch-to-core attention — patches pull
  cores towards semantically rich regions, and the clique interaction between cores encourages diversity.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── §05 -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">05 — Results</div>
<h2>Competitive with the Best Despite No Patch-to-Patch Attention</h2>

<p>
  VECA is trained with feature distillation from a frozen DINOv3 teacher on unlabeled Objects365 images —
  no labels, no task-specific fine-tuning. Evaluation freezes the backbone and trains only a linear head.
</p>

